          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My blood sugar has been over 20 all day and I feel sick. How urgent is that, and what should I be told about diabetic emergencies?",
          "answer": "Diabetic emergencies related to very high blood sugar, such as diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) or hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS), develop over hours to days and are life-threatening. You should call emergency services immediately if you experience very high blood sugar along with any of these features (red-flag-diabetic-emergency):\n\n*   Nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n*   Deep, rapid, or labored breathing, or breath with a fruity or acetone smell.\n*   Drowsiness, confusion, or difficulty staying awake.\n*   Severe dehydration, very dry mouth, or not passing urine.\n*   Rapid breathing and weakness, especially in someone with type 1 diabetes, or anyone unable to keep fluids down.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
